solution.p:
If the point (5,-2) is on y=-\frac{3}{5}x+1\text{,} once we substitute x=5 and y=-2 into the lines equation, the equation should be true. Lets try:
\begin{aligned}
y\amp=-\frac{3}{5}x+1\\
-2\amp\wonder{=}-\frac{3}{5}(5)+1\\
-2\amp\confirm{=} -3+1
\end{aligned}
Because this last equation is true, we can say that (5,-2) is on the graph of y=-\frac{3}{5}x+1\text{.}
